# {{ exercise_title }} — agent guide

**This app has two zones. Edit only one of them.**

- **The seam** (`exercise/**`) — the only per-app code. Python only; it *may* use streamlit.
- **The chassis** (`core/`, `ui/`, `app.py`, deploy config) — identical in every app in
  this family. Do NOT edit it per app.

The app is a single-process **Streamlit** application:

```
core/       framework-free Python. Imports streamlit NOWHERE.
            identity, storage, cohorts, results, export, admin, plot helpers.
ui/         thin Streamlit UI — chassis screens only.
exercise/   THE SEAM: schema.py, capture.py, analysis.py, content.md.
app.py      entry point, course gate, onboarding, navigation.
```

**Never rename `ui/` to `pages/`.** It looks like the Streamlit convention and it is a trap:
Streamlit switches into magic multipage mode from the directory *name* alone
(`Path(main_script_parent / "pages").exists()`). Every module in it then becomes a page with
its own URL and its own sidebar entry — including `login`, `session_url` and `_components`,
which are machinery, not screens, and which would bypass the course gate in `app.py`. This
shipped once and confused students; `tests/test_navigation.py` now fails if `pages/` reappears.

**`core/` must never import streamlit.** That is what keeps the UI swappable and makes
`core/` reusable as a shared library later; `tests/test_core_no_streamlit.py` enforces it and
will fail the build on a stray import. `exercise/schema.py` is imported by `core/`, so it must
stay framework-free too.

